LESSONS["1A"].convo1=[{"q":"How do you think people should act when something stressful happens at work?",
"sample":"Well, honestly, I think it depends a lot on the person, but in general, you should just try to stay calm, you know? Like, when a problem comes up suddenly, a good manager doesn't react hastily — they take a breath and think about it carefully first, instead of just panicking. And they listen patiently to what their team has to say, rather than getting reckless and making a quick decision on their own. I've noticed that if everyone on the team communicates clearly and just works hard together, things usually get resolved pretty easily, even when the deadline's coming up really fast. It's definitely not easy, but staying calm really does make a difference.",
"glossary":{"stay calm":{"fa":"آرام ماندن","type":"colloc"},"react hastily":{"fa":"با عجله واکنش نشان دادن","type":"colloc"}},
"hl":["calm","hastily","carefully","patiently","reckless","clearly","hard","easily","fast"]}];
LESSONS["1A"].convo2=[{"q":"What's it like using public transportation in your city?",
"sample":"Honestly, it really depends on the day. Some people just stand quietly in line and wait patiently, you know, and they talk pretty softly on the phone so they don't bother anyone else around them. But then other people are way louder — they talk really energetically, laugh a lot, and just say whatever they're feeling right out in public, which can be a bit much sometimes. What I've noticed, though, is that if you can adapt quickly to how the people around you are acting, you actually end up enjoying the ride a lot more instead of getting annoyed. I used to get impatient about it, but now I just try to relax and go with it.",
"glossary":{"adapt quickly":{"fa":"سریع خود را وفق دادن","type":"colloc"}},
"hl":["quietly","patiently","softly","energetically","openly","quickly","immensely"]}];
