      question: "Were there many children in your family growing up—what was that like?",
      sample: "There were four of us, so it was noisy, as you can imagine. **Never a dull moment**, honestly. Somebody was always shouting about something, and somebody else was always in the bathroom. We **shared a room**, me and my brother, and there were a lot of **hand-me-downs** going round—I don't think I had a single new pair of shoes until I was about twelve. You'd think that'd bother you as a kid, but it didn't really. It teaches you to share, I suppose, and to fight for the bathroom in the morning. And it teaches you to compromise, because if you don't, nobody gets anything. We were close, though, that's the thing. We had our arguments—proper ones, doors slamming—but nobody from outside could say anything about my brother without me **jumping in**. That was my job, apparently. And you always had someone to play with, which I think only children miss out on. So yeah, it was chaotic and there was never any peace and I never got any privacy, but I **wouldn't have it any other way**. I couldn't do it with four kids myself, mind you. I don't know how my mum did it.\n",
